#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Regenerate the Margin Notes review tool's manuscript content from paper/main.tex.

margin_notes.html carries, inside its main <script>, a region bounded by

    // BEGIN GENERATED by tools/margin_notes/build_doc_from_tex.py -- ...
    const CITES = {...}; function cite(...) {...}; const SECTIONS = [...]; const DOC = [...];
    // END GENERATED

This script rebuilds that region from paper/main.tex (+ main.aux for \\ref numbers,
refs.bib for CITES, numbers.json for \\val) so the DOC array never has to be
hand-mirrored again. META and all app code outside the region stay byte-identical.

Usage (Python 3.11, stdlib only):
    python tools/margin_notes/build_doc_from_tex.py            # --report (default): no writes to
                                                                # margin_notes.html; prints the id
                                                                # carry-forward report and writes
                                                                # generated_preview.html
    python tools/margin_notes/build_doc_from_tex.py --check    # exit 1 if the region is stale, the
                                                                # markers are missing, or generation
                                                                # fails (e.g. unknown \\val key)
    python tools/margin_notes/build_doc_from_tex.py --write [--allow-drop id1,id2]

Comments in the live tool are keyed by block id, so ids are carried forward:
generated blocks are aligned to the current DOC (same type, order-preserving
weighted alignment on word-level difflib similarity >= MATCH_THRESHOLD; then a
second, order-free pass for blocks that moved; footnote-style blocks are aligned
as their own sequence). Matched blocks keep their old id (figures also keep their
old `img`). An existing id that matches nothing is DROPPED (its comments would be
orphaned): --write refuses unless --allow-drop names it, and dropped ids are
recorded in a "retired ids" comment so they are never reused for a new block.
Header comment keys ("h:<id>") count as ids too.

Conventions replicated from the hand-made DOC array
---------------------------------------------------
* Blocks: paragraphs {id, tex, section, h2?, h3?, text}; tables {id, section,
  h2?, h3?, type:'table', label:'Table N', caption, headers, rows}; figures
  {id, section, ..., type:'figure', label:'Figure N', caption, img}.
* `tex` is the block's raw LaTeX (comments removed, whitespace collapsed to one
  line); `text`/`caption` are HTML template literals.  (The hand mirror had
  partially pre-rendered its tex fields; this generator keeps them raw.)
* Abstract: one block id 'abstract', section 'front', h2 'Abstract'.
* \\section -> h2, \\subsection/\\subsubsection -> h3, attached to the first
  block of that (sub)section (both keys when a section opens with a
  subsection; the app shows the h2).  Header carry-forward: if a float precedes
  the first paragraph and the old DOC put that header on a later block (e.g.
  'results-k4-1'), the header stays on that block so "h:<id>" keys survive.
  Section nav ids come from \\label{sec:<id>} (existing SECTIONS ids/labels are
  kept; new sections derive id/label).  Appendix h2 = "Appendix X: <title>".
* Paragraph = blank-line-separated chunk.  itemize and description stay inline
  in their paragraph (items joined by spaces; description items render as
  "<strong>label</strong>: body").  enumerate in the defects section is split
  one block per item, with a bold "Defect N: " lead inserted into the item's
  leading \\textbf (not literally in main.tex).  \\paragraph{X} -> bold lead.
* \\footnote{...} is removed from its block's text (no marker) and becomes a
  separate block right after it: id '<parent>-fn<k>', tex '[footnote] <raw>',
  text '<span class="footnote-block">Footnote: ...</span>'.  Appendix
  description items that reference a table (\\item[Note a (Table~\\ref{tab:x},
  ...)]) are shown the same way right after that table ('<table-id>-fn<k>',
  tex '[appendix note] ...'); the appendix keeps only its remaining prose.
* \\todo{x}/\\pending{x} -> <span class="editorial-flag">[TODO: x]</span> /
  [PENDING: x].
* Inline: \\emph/\\textit -> <em>, \\textbf -> <strong>, \\texttt -> <code>,
  -- -> &ndash;, --- -> &mdash;, ``x'' -> "x", ~ -> space, \\% \\_ \\& \\{ \\} escapes,
  {,} -> ",", < > & HTML-escaped, \\url{u} -> u.
* Math is flattened to plain text: $p=0.0015$ -> p=0.0015, $^\\dagger$ -> †,
  $\\times$ -> x, $\\sim$ -> ~, $\\ll$ -> "much less than", \\text{..} unwrapped,
  x_\\text{eff} -> x_eff, other superscripts -> <sup>..</sup>.
* \\citep/\\citet/\\cite{a,b} -> ${cite('a','b')} inside template literals (table
  cells, being plain strings, get the rendered <cite> HTML).  CITES short names
  come from refs.bib: "Last YEAR", "Last & Last YEAR", "Last et al. YEAR".
* \\ref/\\Cref resolve to main.aux numbers ('??' + warning when missing).
* \\val{key} renders numbers.json values[key].value (LaTeX, e.g. 2{,}000 ->
  2,000); a missing file/key is a hard error naming the key.  \\claim{id}{text}
  renders as text (the tex field keeps it verbatim).
* Tables: first tabular row = headers; \\multirow{n}{*}{x} -> x on its first
  row, '' below; rules/\\addlinespace dropped; an empty first cell repeats the
  row-group's label (the hand mirror's "Bibliography entry" x2 rows).
* The refs.bib table (label 'refs.bib') is generated from refs.bib at
  \\bibliography's position.
* Hand-authored blocks with no LaTeX counterpart (HAND_AUTHORED_IDS) are kept
  verbatim (raw source), placed after the block that preceded them before.
"""
